WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:06.080
The examples of code you see in this course&nbsp;
are short, simple code snippets used either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:14.400
alone or as part of a larger, more robust&nbsp;
program. Code, or the purpose of code written,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.320
should be understandable, even to people&nbsp;
unfamiliar with the intent of the code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.200
Comments are text that follows the number symbol,&nbsp;
either on a line by itself or at the end of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:33.160
line of code. Python will ignore this text during&nbsp;
execution, allowing you to annotate your code for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:40.960
better understanding. I have this 413-comments&nbsp;
file open and will add a comment on line 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:47.880
above the while loop, explaining what the&nbsp;
while loop does. These explanations can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.160
help one remember what code is supposed to&nbsp;
do, which can help build proper game flows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:58.000
and comments can help others understand the&nbsp;
purpose of code. Comments can also be inline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.560
meaning they can be placed to the right of a&nbsp;
line, and anything after the comment is ignored.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:11.960
Comments do not split lines, however, so each&nbsp;
line can only have one inline comment. I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:18.120
add a comment to the end of line 6 explaining why&nbsp;
we need to set the range to 11 when the loop runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:25.360
10 times. Finally, and this is something&nbsp;
we have seen in the course, a comment can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:31.400
comment out a code statement or line of code,&nbsp;
meaning that the code is legitimate but needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:36.760
to be ignored temporarily. Lines are commented&nbsp;
out when developers want to test blocks of code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:42.680
with minimal distractions and thus pinpoint&nbsp;
potential problems more easily. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.760
the print statement on line 10 is unnecessary for&nbsp;
testing this code. Therefore, I will add a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:55.480
symbol in front of the code on line 10, run this&nbsp;
code, and the code executes but does not print the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:01.280
thank you statement on line 10 as it has been&nbsp;
commented out. Python doesn't natively support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:07.160
block comments, multi-line comments, like&nbsp;
some other languages, but in the next topic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:14.360
we will see how we can get the equivalent of a&nbsp;
multi-line comment through the use of docstrings.

